/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Ezra chapters 6-10
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("ezra", [
{n:6, name:"The Temple Finished", seal:{ref:"Ezra 6:16",verse:"And the children of Israel, the priests, and the Levites, and the rest of the children of the captivity, kept the dedication of this house of God with joy,"}, facts:["Did you know the decree that saved the temple work was found not in Babylon but in a roll at Achmetha, the palace in the province of the Medes?","Did you know Darius ordered the temple paid for out of the royal tribute, turning the enemies' complaint into the builders' funding?","Did you know the temple was finished in the month of Adar in the sixth year of Darius, about twenty years after the foundation was first laid?"], questions:[
{q:"Where was the roll found that contained the decree of Cyrus?", options:["At Achmetha, in the palace in the province of the Medes","At Babylon, in the house of the king's treasure","At Susa, in the royal archives","At Jerusalem, in the temple courts"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:2",verse:"And there was found at Achmetha, in the palace that is in the province of the Medes, a roll, and therein was a record thus written:", insight:"God kept the proof of His promise safe in a foreign palace until the exact moment His people needed it."},
{q:"What did Darius do when the temple work was challenged?", options:["He commanded a search of the house of the rolls","He ordered the work to stop at once","He arrested the Jewish elders","He wrote a new law against building"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:1",verse:"Then Darius the king made a decree, and search was made in the house of the rolls, where the treasures were laid up in Babylon.", insight:"When God's work is questioned, even a pagan king can become the instrument that vindicates it."},
{q:"In which year of his reign did Cyrus make the decree to build the house of God?", options:["In the first year of Cyrus the king","In the seventh year of Cyrus","In the last year of his reign","The year is never stated"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:3",verse:"In the first year of Cyrus the king the same Cyrus the king made a decree concerning the house of God at Jerusalem, Let the house be builded, the place where they offered sacrifices, and let the foundations thereof be strongly laid; the height thereof threescore cubits, and the breadth thereof threescore cubits;", insight:"God moved Cyrus in his very first year, showing that His purposes do not wait on human timing."},
{q:"According to the decree, how high was the new house to be?", options:["Threescore cubits","Fifty cubits","An hundred cubits","Forty cubits"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:3",verse:"In the first year of Cyrus the king the same Cyrus the king made a decree concerning the house of God at Jerusalem, Let the house be builded, the place where they offered sacrifices, and let the foundations thereof be strongly laid; the height thereof threescore cubits, and the breadth thereof threescore cubits;", insight:"God's house was to be built to a royal scale, because the King it honored deserved nothing small."},
{q:"How was the temple wall to be constructed?", options:["Three rows of great stones, and a row of new timber","Seven courses of brick with iron bands","Entirely of cedar from Lebanon","Of marble brought from Greece"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:4",verse:"With three rows of great stones, and a row of new timber: and let the expences be given out of the king’s house:", insight:"Even the building method was written into the decree, a reminder that details matter to God."},
{q:"What was to become of the gold and silver vessels Nebuchadnezzar had taken?", options:["They were to be restored and brought again unto the temple","They were to be melted into Persian coin","They were to remain in Babylon forever","They were to be divided among the priests"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:5",verse:"And also let the golden and silver vessels of the house of God, which Nebuchadnezzar took forth out of the temple which is at Jerusalem, and brought unto Babylon, be restored, and brought again unto the temple which is at Jerusalem, every one to his place, and place them in the house of God.", insight:"What was taken in judgment is restored in mercy. God reclaims what belongs to Him."},
{q:"Whom did Darius order to stay away from the temple work?", options:["Tatnai, governor beyond the river, with Shetharboznai and their companions","Zerubbabel and Jeshua","The elders of the Jews","The Levites and singers"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:6",verse:"Now therefore, Tatnai, governor beyond the river, Shethar-boznai, and your companions the Apharsachites, which are beyond the river, be ye far from thence:", insight:"God can silence opposition with a single royal sentence. The critics were told to keep their distance."},
{q:"What was Darius's command concerning the Jewish elders?", options:["Let the work of this house of God alone","Finish it within twelve months","Guard it with soldiers","Tear down what was built"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:7",verse:"Let the work of this house of God alone; let the governor of the Jews and the elders of the Jews build this house of God in his place.", insight:"The highest power on earth commanded the work to be left alone. No one overrules God."},
{q:"How were the building expenses to be paid?", options:["Out of the king's goods, from the tribute beyond the river","By a new tax on the returned exiles","From the temple treasury only","By selling the gold vessels"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:8",verse:"Moreover I make a decree what ye shall do to the elders of these Jews for the building of this house of God: that of the king’s goods, even of the tribute beyond the river, forthwith expenses be given unto these men, that they be not hindered.", insight:"God made the empire's tax revenue fund His temple. He provides from the most unlikely purses."},
{q:"Which supplies were to be given to the priests day by day without fail?", options:["Bullocks, rams, lambs, wheat, salt, wine, and oil","Figs, honey, and spices","Fine linen and purple cloth","Camels, horses, and chariots"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:9",verse:"And that which they have need of, both young bullocks, and rams, and lambs, for the burnt offerings of the God of heaven, wheat, salt, wine, and oil, according to the appointment of the priests which are at Jerusalem, let it be given them day by day without fail:", insight:"God's servants were not to lack a single day's provision. Daily needs deserve daily trust."},
{q:"Why were these supplies given to the priests?", options:["That they might offer sacrifices of sweet savour and pray for the life of the king and his sons","That they might feed the poor of Jerusalem","That they might trade with the nations","That they might enrich themselves"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:10",verse:"That they may offer sacrifices of sweet savours unto the God of heaven, and pray for the life of the king, and of his sons.", insight:"Prayer for rulers is part of worship. The church's intercession blesses even kings who do not know God."},
{q:"What punishment awaited anyone who altered the king's decree?", options:["Timber pulled from his house, he hanged thereon, and his house made a dunghill","Exile to a distant province","Forty stripes with a rod","A fine of a hundred talents"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:11",verse:"Also I have made a decree, that whosoever shall alter this word, let timber be pulled down from his house, and being set up, let him be hanged thereon; and let his house be made a dunghill for this.", insight:"Darius guarded God's house with terrible severity, a shadow of how seriously heaven guards its own."},
{q:"What did Darius declare God would do to any king or people who destroyed the house?", options:["God would destroy them","God would forgive them","God would enrich them","He said nothing about it"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:12",verse:"And the God that hath caused his name to dwell there destroy all kings and people, that shall put to their hand to alter and to destroy this house of God which is at Jerusalem. I Darius have made a decree; let it be done with speed.", insight:"Even a heathen king confessed that God defends His dwelling. Truth can come from unexpected lips."},
{q:"How did Tatnai and his companions respond to the decree?", options:["They did speedily as Darius the king had sent","They delayed for many months","They appealed to a higher court","They fled across the river"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:13",verse:"Then Tatnai, governor on this side the river, Shethar-boznai, and their companions, according to that which Darius the king had sent, so they did speedily.", insight:"Opposition turned to obedience in a day. When God speaks through authority, resistance melts."},
{q:"Through whose prophesying did the elders prosper as they built?", options:["Haggai the prophet and Zechariah the son of Iddo","Isaiah and Jeremiah","Ezekiel and Daniel","Malachi and Joel"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:14",verse:"And the elders of the Jews builded, and they prospered through the prophesying of Haggai the prophet and Zechariah the son of Iddo. And they builded, and finished it, according to the commandment of the God of Israel, and according to the commandment of Cyrus, and Darius, and Artaxerxes king of Persia.", insight:"Building prospers when preaching accompanies it. God's word and God's work belong together."},
{q:"When was the house finished?", options:["On the third day of the month Adar, in the sixth year of Darius","On the first day of Nisan, in the first year of Cyrus","On the fourteenth day of the first month","On the twenty-fifth day of the ninth month"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:15",verse:"And this house was finished on the third day of the month Adar, which was in the sixth year of the reign of Darius the king.", insight:"After years of delay and opposition, the work was finished on an ordinary day that heaven had marked."},
{q:"How did the children of Israel keep the dedication of the house?", options:["With joy","In solemn silence","With fasting and weeping","In fear"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:16",verse:"And the children of Israel, the priests, and the Levites, and the rest of the children of the captivity, kept the dedication of this house of God with joy,", insight:"Dedication ended in joy, not mere relief. Finishing God's work is a cause for celebration."},
{q:"How many he goats were offered as a sin offering at the dedication?", options:["Twelve, according to the number of the tribes of Israel","Seven, for the days of the week","Seventy, for the elders","One, for the high priest"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:17",verse:"And offered at the dedication of this house of God an hundred bullocks, two hundred rams, four hundred lambs; and for a sin offering for all Israel, twelve he goats, according to the number of the tribes of Israel.", insight:"Twelve goats for twelve tribes: the whole nation, reunited, confessed together."},
{q:"When did the children of the captivity keep the Passover?", options:["Upon the fourteenth day of the first month","Upon the tenth day of the seventh month","Upon the fifteenth day of Adar","Upon the first day of the year"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:19",verse:"And the children of the captivity kept the passover upon the fourteenth day of the first month.", insight:"The Passover returned to a rebuilt temple. Redemption remembered is redemption renewed."},
{q:"How long did they keep the feast of unleavened bread, and why with joy?", options:["Seven days, for the LORD had made them joyful and turned the heart of the king of Assyria unto them","Three days, because the work was done","Forty days, as in the wilderness","One day, as a memorial"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 6:22",verse:"And kept the feast of unleavened bread seven days with joy: for the LORD had made them joyful, and turned the heart of the king of Assyria unto them, to strengthen their hands in the work of the house of God, the God of Israel.", insight:"Their joy had a source: God had turned a king's heart. Changed hearts, even royal ones, are His doing."}
]},
{n:7, name:"Ezra Goes Up", seal:{ref:"Ezra 7:10",verse:"For Ezra had prepared his heart to seek the law of the LORD, and to do it, and to teach in Israel statutes and judgments."}, facts:["Did you know Ezra was both a priest and a scribe, a descendant of Aaron, trained in the law of Moses before he ever led a journey?","Did you know the journey from Babylon to Jerusalem took about four months, from the first day of the first month to the first day of the fifth month?","Did you know Artaxerxes called himself 'king of kings' yet exempted God's ministers from his own tolls and taxes?"], questions:[
{q:"How does the genealogy introduce Ezra?", options:["Ezra the son of Seraiah, the son of Azariah","Ezra the son of Azariah, the son of Seraiah","Ezra the son of Hilkiah the high priest","Ezra the son of Jozadak"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:1",verse:"Now after these things, in the reign of Artaxerxes king of Persia, Ezra the son of Seraiah, the son of Azariah, the son of Hilkiah,", insight:"Ezra's lineage is traced with care because his calling was priestly. God prepares His servants long before He sends them."},
{q:"What kind of scribe was Ezra?", options:["A ready scribe in the law of Moses","A scribe of the king's chronicles","A scribe skilled in the Chaldean tongue","A scribe of military records"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:6",verse:"This Ezra went up from Babylon; and he was a ready scribe in the law of Moses, which the LORD God of Israel had given: and the king granted him all his request, according to the hand of the LORD his God upon him.", insight:"Ezra's readiness was in Scripture, not in court politics. Skill in God's word is the best preparation."},
{q:"Who went up with Ezra to Jerusalem?", options:["Some of the children of Israel, with priests, Levites, singers, porters, and Nethinims","Ezra and his household alone","The Persian royal guard","Only the sons of Levi"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:7",verse:"And there went up some of the children of Israel, and of the priests, and the Levites, and the singers, and the porters, and the Nethinims, unto Jerusalem, in the seventh year of Artaxerxes the king.", insight:"It was a mixed company, every class of God's people moving together. Revival travels in community."},
{q:"In which year of Artaxerxes did Ezra go up?", options:["The seventh year","The first year","The twentieth year","The twelfth year"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:7",verse:"And there went up some of the children of Israel, and of the priests, and the Levites, and the singers, and the porters, and the Nethinims, unto Jerusalem, in the seventh year of Artaxerxes the king.", insight:"God's timing put Ezra on the road in the king's seventh year, neither hurried nor delayed."},
{q:"In what month did Ezra reach Jerusalem?", options:["The fifth month","The first month","The seventh month","The ninth month"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:8",verse:"And he came to Jerusalem in the fifth month, which was in the seventh year of the king.", insight:"Four months on the road, and the good hand of God on every mile."},
{q:"When did Ezra begin the journey up from Babylon?", options:["Upon the first day of the first month","Upon the twelfth day of the first month","Upon the first day of the seventh month","Upon the tenth day of the fifth month"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:9",verse:"For upon the first day of the first month began he to go up from Babylon, and on the first day of the fifth month came he to Jerusalem, according to the good hand of his God upon him.", insight:"He began on the first day of the year, a new beginning marked on the calendar of faith."},
{q:"What three things had Ezra prepared his heart to do?", options:["To seek the law of the LORD, and to do it, and to teach in Israel statutes and judgments","To build the walls, gather taxes, and number the people","To write chronicles, judge cases, and lead armies","To pray, fast, and offer sacrifices"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:10",verse:"For Ezra had prepared his heart to seek the law of the LORD, and to do it, and to teach in Israel statutes and judgments.", insight:"Study, obedience, teaching: in that order. Ezra practiced before he preached."},
{q:"How does Artaxerxes address Ezra in the letter?", options:["Ezra the priest, a scribe of the law of the God of heaven","Ezra the governor of Judah","Ezra the keeper of the royal treasury","Ezra the prophet of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:12",verse:"Artaxerxes, king of kings, unto Ezra the priest, a scribe of the law of the God of heaven, perfect peace, and at such a time.", insight:"A pagan king recognized Ezra's true office. A life devoted to Scripture earns respect even from unbelievers."},
{q:"Who was free to go up to Jerusalem under the king's decree?", options:["All of the people of Israel, and of his priests and Levites, who were minded of their own freewill to go","Only those born in Jerusalem","Only the priests and their sons","The king's own servants"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:13",verse:"I make a decree, that all they of the people of Israel, and of his priests and Levites, in my realm, which are minded of their own freewill to go up to Jerusalem, go with thee.", insight:"The decree opened the door but forced no one through it. God honors the willing heart."},
{q:"Why was Ezra sent to Judah and Jerusalem?", options:["To enquire concerning Judah and Jerusalem, according to the law of his God","To collect the king's tribute","To build a palace for Artaxerxes","To take a census of the people"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:14",verse:"Forasmuch as thou art sent of the king, and of his seven counsellors, to enquire concerning Judah and Jerusalem, according to the law of thy God which is in thine hand;", insight:"His mission was measured by Scripture, not by politics. God's law was his yardstick."},
{q:"What silver and gold was Ezra to carry?", options:["All he could find in the province of Babylon, plus what the king and his counsellors freely offered","Only the temple tax of one year","The spoils of the Egyptian campaign","The king's personal wages for a year"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:16",verse:"And all the silver and gold that thou canst find in all the province of Babylon, with the freewill offering of the people, and of the priests, offering willingly for the house of their God which is in Jerusalem:", insight:"Freewill offerings from a heathen court, gathered for God's house. God moves hearts that do not even know Him."},
{q:"What was Ezra to buy speedily with the money?", options:["Bullocks, rams, and lambs, with their offerings, to offer upon the altar","Horses and chariots for the journey","Timber from Lebanon","Fields around Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:17",verse:"That thou mayest buy speedily with this money bullocks, rams, lambs, with their meat offerings and their drink offerings, and offer them upon the altar of the house of your God which is in Jerusalem.", insight:"The silver was for sacrifices, not for comfort. Worship came before everything."},
{q:"What was to be done with the rest of the silver and gold?", options:["Whatsoever seemed good to Ezra and his brethren, after the will of God","It must all be returned to the king","It was to be buried for safety","It was to be divided as wages"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:18",verse:"And whatsoever shall seem good to thee, and to thy brethren, to do with the rest of the silver and the gold, that do after the will of your God.", insight:"God trusted Ezra's judgment. Faithfulness with much begins with faithfulness in God's house."},
{q:"What was Ezra to do with the vessels for the service of the house of God?", options:["Deliver them before the God of Jerusalem","Keep them safe in Babylon","Sell them to buy food","Melt them into coin"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:19",verse:"The vessels also that are given thee for the service of the house of thy God, those deliver thou before the God of Jerusalem.", insight:"Holy things belong in God's presence. Ezra carried them as a sacred trust."},
{q:"What did Artaxerxes command the treasurers beyond the river?", options:["Whatsoever Ezra required of them, it be done speedily","Give him only a tenth of his request","Give him food but no silver","Send soldiers instead of supplies"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:21",verse:"And I, even I Artaxerxes the king, do make a decree to all the treasurers which are beyond the river, that whatsoever Ezra the priest, the scribe of the law of the God of heaven, shall require of you, it be done speedily,", insight:"The empire's treasuries stood open at Ezra's word. When God favors His servant, resources follow."},
{q:"What were the limits set for the treasurers?", options:["An hundred talents of silver, an hundred measures of wheat, an hundred baths of wine, an hundred baths of oil, and salt without prescribing how much","Ten of each, and no more","Silver only, and no food","Whatever each treasurer thought fair"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:22",verse:"Unto an hundred talents of silver, and to an hundred measures of wheat, and to an hundred baths of wine, and to an hundred baths of oil, and salt without prescribing how much.", insight:"Generosity with boundaries: even royal giving was ordered and sufficient, never wasteful."},
{q:"Which servants of the temple were freed from toll, tribute, and custom?", options:["The priests, Levites, singers, porters, Nethinims, and ministers of the house of God","Only the high priest","The king's own officials","The merchants of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:24",verse:"Also we certify you, that touching any of the priests and Levites, singers, porters, Nethinims, or ministers of this house of God, it shall not be lawful to impose toll, tribute, or custom, upon them.", insight:"Those who serve God's house are exempt from the world's taxes. Heaven honors its workers."},
{q:"What was Ezra to set over the people beyond the river?", options:["Magistrates and judges, to judge all the people","Captains over the army","Collectors of the tribute","Overseers of the building"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:25",verse:"And thou, Ezra, after the wisdom of thy God, that is in thine hand, set magistrates and judges, which may judge all the people that are beyond the river, all such as know the laws of thy God; and teach ye them that know them not.", insight:"Justice was part of revival. God's people were to be governed by God's law."},
{q:"What was the penalty for refusing the law of God and the law of the king?", options:["Death, banishment, confiscation of goods, or imprisonment","A fine of silver","Exclusion from the temple","Thirty days of fasting"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:26",verse:"And whosoever will not do the law of thy God, and the law of the king, let judgment be executed speedily upon him, whether it be unto death, or to banishment, or to confiscation of goods, or to imprisonment.", insight:"The king's sword backed God's law. Obedience was not treated as optional."},
{q:"For what did Ezra bless the LORD concerning the king's heart?", options:["That God had put it in the king's heart to beautify the house of the LORD","That the king had made Ezra governor","That all the captives were freed","That war had ended"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 7:27",verse:"Blessed be the LORD God of our fathers, which hath put such a thing as this in the king’s heart, to beautify the house of the LORD which is in Jerusalem:", insight:"Ezra saw past the king to the God who moved him. Every favor traces back to heaven."}
]},
{n:8, name:"The Journey to Jerusalem", seal:{ref:"Ezra 8:31",verse:"Then we departed from the river of Ahava on the twelfth day of the first month, to go unto Jerusalem: and the hand of our God was upon us, and he delivered us from the hand of the enemy, and of such as lay in wait by the way."}, facts:["Did you know Ezra refused a military escort because he had told the king that God's hand protects those who seek Him, so he staked the whole journey on prayer and fasting instead?","Did you know the silver alone weighed 650 talents, an enormous fortune carried across dangerous roads by twelve priests?","Did you know the returning company numbered about fifteen hundred men, plus their families, all gathered at the river of Ahava?"], questions:[
{q:"What does chapter 8 open with?", options:["The chief of the fathers, and the genealogy of those who went up with Ezra","The dedication of the temple","The keeping of the Passover","The numbering of the vessels"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:1",verse:"These are now the chief of their fathers, and this is the genealogy of them that went up with me from Babylon, in the reign of Artaxerxes the king.", insight:"God records the names of those who go with Him. Every willing traveler is written down."},
{q:"Which descendant of David went up with Ezra?", options:["Hattush","Zerubbabel","Daniel","Gershom"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:2",verse:"Of the sons of Phinehas; Gershom: of the sons of Ithamar; Daniel: of the sons of David; Hattush.", insight:"The royal line was still among the returners. God's promises to David had not been forgotten."},
{q:"Where did Ezra gather the company?", options:["At the river that runneth to Ahava","At the river Euphrates","At the gates of Babylon","At the river Jordan"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:15",verse:"And I gathered them together to the river than runneth to Ahava; and there abode we in tents three days: and I viewed the people, and the priests, and found there none of the sons of Levi.", insight:"Before the journey, a gathering. God's people prepare together before they move together."},
{q:"When they reached Jerusalem, how long did they rest before the treasure was weighed?", options:["Three days","Seven days","One day","Forty days"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:32",verse:"And we came to Jerusalem, and abode there three days.", insight:"After the long road, three days of rest. Even holy work needs a pause before the next task."},
{q:"What was missing when Ezra viewed the people and the priests?", options:["None of the sons of Levi were there","There were no singers","There was no gold","There were no children"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:15",verse:"And I gathered them together to the river than runneth to Ahava; and there abode we in tents three days: and I viewed the people, and the priests, and found there none of the sons of Levi.", insight:"Ezra noticed the gap that mattered: no Levites for God's house. Leaders see what is missing, not just what is present."},
{q:"Whom did Ezra send to find ministers for the house of God?", options:["Chief men, men of understanding, with a message for Iddo at Casiphia","His own sons, traveling alone","Officers of the Persian king","The Nethinims already with him"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:16",verse:"Then sent I for Eliezer, for Ariel, for Shemaiah, and for Elnathan, and for Jarib, and for Elnathan, and for Nathan, and for Zechariah, and for Meshullam, chief men; also for Joiarib, and for Elnathan, men of understanding.", insight:"When workers were lacking, Ezra sent his best men to find them. Recruiting for God's work deserves our best effort."},
{q:"What was the message for Iddo the chief at Casiphia?", options:["That they should bring unto us ministers for the house of our God","That they should bring an army for protection","That they should bring the king's tribute","That they should remain where they were"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:17",verse:"And I sent them with commandment unto Iddo the chief at the place Casiphia, and I told them what they should say unto Iddo, and to his brethren the Nethinims, at the place Casiphia, that they should bring unto us ministers for the house of our God.", insight:"The request was specific: ministers for God's house. Clear asking is part of faithful leading."},
{q:"How many Nethinims came, all expressed by name?", options:["Two hundred and twenty","One hundred","Seventy","Three hundred"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:20",verse:"Also of the Nethinims, whom David and the princes had appointed for the service of the Levites, two hundred and twenty Nethinims: all of them were expressed by name.", insight:"Every one was expressed by name. In God's service, nobody is just a number."},
{q:"Why did Ezra proclaim a fast at the river of Ahava?", options:["To seek of God a right way for them, their little ones, and all their substance","To mourn those who had died","To prepare for battle","To mark the new moon"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:21",verse:"Then I proclaimed a fast there, at the river of Ahava, that we might afflict ourselves before our God, to seek of him a right way for us, and for our little ones, and for all our substance.", insight:"Before the road, they bowed the knee. The safest journey begins with fasting and prayer."},
{q:"Why was Ezra ashamed to ask the king for soldiers and horsemen?", options:["Because he had told the king that the hand of God is upon all them for good that seek Him","Because the king was angry with him","Because he had no gift worthy of the king","Because the road was short and safe"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:22",verse:"For I was ashamed to require of the king a band of soldiers and horsemen to help us against the enemy in the way: because we had spoken unto the king, saying, The hand of our God is upon all them for good that seek him; but his power and his wrath is against all them that forsake him.", insight:"Ezra had boasted in God's protection, so he would not contradict his testimony. Our words about God should shape our actions."},
{q:"What came of their fasting and prayer?", options:["He was intreated of us","The king sent soldiers after all","They turned back to Babylon","The record does not say"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:23",verse:"So we fasted and besought our God for this: and he was intreated of us.", insight:"God heard. The God who is sought in fasting is the God who answers."},
{q:"How many chief priests did Ezra separate to carry the treasure?", options:["Twelve","Seven","Seventy","Thirty"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:24",verse:"Then I separated twelve of the chief of the priests, Sherebiah, Hashabiah, and ten of their brethren with them,", insight:"Twelve priests for a holy trust. Sacred things are carried by consecrated hands."},
{q:"How much silver did Ezra weigh into their hands?", options:["Six hundred and fifty talents","One hundred talents","A thousand talents","Fifty talents"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:26",verse:"I even weighed unto their hand six hundred and fifty talents of silver, and silver vessels an hundred talents, and of gold an hundred talents;", insight:"An immense sum, weighed out in the open. God's wealth is handled with exactness."},
{q:"What precious vessels are named with the silver and gold?", options:["Twenty basons of gold, and two vessels of fine copper, precious as gold","Cups of onyx and crystal","Shields of beaten iron","Boxes of carved ivory"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:27",verse:"Also twenty basons of gold, of a thousand drams; and two vessels of fine copper, precious as gold.", insight:"Even copper was precious as gold when dedicated to God. Consecration gives value to common things."},
{q:"What did Ezra say to the priests as he gave them the treasure?", options:["Ye are holy unto the LORD; the vessels are holy also","Hide it from every robber","Spend it before we reach Jerusalem","Count it only once"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:28",verse:"And I said unto them, Ye are holy unto the LORD; the vessels are holy also; and the silver and the gold are a freewill offering unto the LORD God of your fathers.", insight:"Holy people carrying holy things: the carriers matter as much as the cargo."},
{q:"Until when were they to watch and keep the treasure?", options:["Until they weighed it before the chief priests and Levites at Jerusalem, in the chambers of the house of the LORD","Until the next Passover","Until Ezra returned to Babylon","Until the king sent for it"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:29",verse:"Watch ye, and keep them, until ye weigh them before the chief of the priests and the Levites, and chief of the fathers of Israel, at Jerusalem, in the chambers of the house of the LORD.", insight:"The trust had a destination: God's house. Faithfulness finishes the delivery."},
{q:"Where did Ezra's company arrive after the journey, and how long did they rest there?", options:["Jerusalem, three days","Babylon, seven days","Ahava, one month","Riblah, forty days"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:32",verse:"And we came to Jerusalem, and abode there three days.", insight:"They came to Jerusalem and rested three days. After every God-guarded journey, pause and breathe."},
{q:"How did God guard them on the way?", options:["He delivered them from the enemy, and from such as lay in wait by the way","He sent a pillar of fire before them","He parted the river for them","He sent Persian horsemen"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:31",verse:"Then we departed from the river of Ahava on the twelfth day of the first month, to go unto Jerusalem: and the hand of our God was upon us, and he delivered us from the hand of the enemy, and of such as lay in wait by the way.", insight:"No escort of soldiers, yet no harm touched them. God's hand was their guard."},
{q:"What did the returned exiles offer at Jerusalem?", options:["Twelve bullocks, ninety and six rams, seventy and seven lambs, and twelve he goats","Only a small thank offering","A thousand doves","Nothing, for they were poor"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:35",verse:"Also the children of those that had been carried away, which were come out of the captivity, offered burnt offerings unto the God of Israel, twelve bullocks for all Israel, ninety and six rams, seventy and seven lambs, twelve he goats for a sin offering: all this was a burnt offering unto the LORD.", insight:"Arriving safely, their first act was worship. Gratitude gives before it rests."},
{q:"What did they do with the king's commissions?", options:["They delivered them to the king's lieutenants and governors, who furthered the people and the house of God","They burned them at the altar","They hid them in the temple","They sent them back to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 8:36",verse:"And they delivered the king’s commissions unto the king’s lieutenants, and to the governors on this side the river: and they furthered the people, and the house of God.", insight:"Even royal paperwork served God's purpose. Every resource, sacred or secular, furthered His house."}
]},
{n:9, name:"Ezra's Confession", seal:{ref:"Ezra 9:6",verse:"And said, O my God, I am ashamed and blush to lift up my face to thee, my God: for our iniquities are increased over our head, and our trespass is grown up unto the heavens."}, facts:["Did you know Ezra's prayer in this chapter is one of the great confessional prayers of the Bible, and he includes himself in the sin though he was personally innocent?","Did you know the phrase 'a nail in his holy place' pictures a tent peg, a small but secure hold that God gave His people in Jerusalem?","Did you know those who 'trembled at the words of God' gathered to Ezra, showing that reverence for Scripture marked the faithful remnant?"], questions:[
{q:"Who came to Ezra with the troubling report?", options:["The princes","The priests","The Levites","The Nethinims"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:1",verse:"Now when these things were done, the princes came to me, saying, The people of Israel, and the priests, and the Levites, have not separated themselves from the people of the lands, doing according to their abominations, even of the Canaanites, the Hittites, the Perizzites, the Jebusites, the Ammonites, the Moabites, the Egyptians, and the Amorites.", insight:"It took courage for the princes to report a sin they shared in. Honest confession starts with honest reporting."},
{q:"What had the people, the priests, and the Levites failed to do?", options:["They had not separated themselves from the people of the lands","They had not paid the temple tax","They had not kept the sabbaths","They had not repaired the wall"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:1",verse:"Now when these things were done, the princes came to me, saying, The people of Israel, and the priests, and the Levites, have not separated themselves from the people of the lands, doing according to their abominations, even of the Canaanites, the Hittites, the Perizzites, the Jebusites, the Ammonites, the Moabites, the Egyptians, and the Amorites.", insight:"Separation was not pride but obedience. God called His people to be distinct in holiness, not distant in arrogance."},
{q:"What was the specific trespass?", options:["They had taken of their daughters for themselves and for their sons","They had stolen the temple vessels","They had offered strange fire","They had refused to work on the temple"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:2",verse:"For they have taken of their daughters for themselves, and for their sons: so that the holy seed have mingled themselves with the people of those lands: yea, the hand of the princes and rulers hath been chief in this trespass.", insight:"Marriage shapes worship. God guarded His people's homes because homes shape hearts."},
{q:"How does Ezra describe the mingling?", options:["The holy seed have mingled themselves with the people of those lands","The salt had lost its savour","The vine had brought forth wild grapes","The leaven had spread through the whole lump"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:2",verse:"For they have taken of their daughters for themselves, and for their sons: so that the holy seed have mingled themselves with the people of those lands: yea, the hand of the princes and rulers hath been chief in this trespass.", insight:"Holy seed was their identity, and they had blurred it. Forgetting who you are is the first step toward losing your way."},
{q:"What did Ezra do when he heard the report?", options:["He rent his garment and his mantle, plucked off the hair of his head and beard, and sat down astonied","He laughed it off as a small matter","He fled back to Babylon","He wrote an angry letter to the king"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:3",verse:"And when I heard this thing, I rent my garment and my mantle, and plucked off the hair of my head and of my beard, and sat down astonied.", insight:"Ezra's grief was physical because his love for God's honor was real. Holy sorrow is never casual."},
{q:"Who gathered to Ezra as he sat astonied?", options:["Every one that trembled at the words of the God of Israel","The king's soldiers","The merchants of Jerusalem","Only the children"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:4",verse:"Then were assembled unto me every one that trembled at the words of the God of Israel, because of the transgression of those that had been carried away; and I sat astonied until the evening sacrifice.", insight:"The trembling ones found each other. Reverence for God's word gathers the faithful remnant."},
{q:"How long did Ezra sit astonied?", options:["Until the evening sacrifice","Until midnight","Until the morning sacrifice","For seven days"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:4",verse:"Then were assembled unto me every one that trembled at the words of the God of Israel, because of the transgression of those that had been carried away; and I sat astonied until the evening sacrifice.", insight:"He sat in stunned silence until the hour of worship. Grief found its way to the altar."},
{q:"What did Ezra do at the evening sacrifice?", options:["He fell upon his knees and spread out his hands unto the LORD his God","He stood with his arms folded","He lay face down without a word","He covered his head and wept silently"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:5",verse:"And at the evening sacrifice I arose up from my heaviness; and having rent my garment and my mantle, I fell upon my knees, and spread out my hands unto the LORD my God.", insight:"Knees bent and hands spread: the posture of a man with nothing to hide and everything to confess."},
{q:"How did Ezra begin his prayer?", options:["O my God, I am ashamed and blush to lift up my face to thee","Hear me, for I am righteous","Why hast thou forsaken thy people","Blessed be thy name forever"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:6",verse:"And said, O my God, I am ashamed and blush to lift up my face to thee, my God: for our iniquities are increased over our head, and our trespass is grown up unto the heavens.", insight:"True confession begins with shame, not excuses. Ezra could barely lift his face, and that is where mercy meets us."},
{q:"What did Ezra say God in His grace had left them?", options:["A remnant to escape","A new temple","A mighty army","Great riches"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:8",verse:"And now for a little space grace hath been shewed from the LORD our God, to leave us a remnant to escape, and to give us a nail in his holy place, that our God may lighten our eyes, and give us a little reviving in our bondage.", insight:"For a little space, grace: a remnant to escape. Even in judgment, God leaves a doorway of mercy."},
{q:"How long had they been in great trespass?", options:["Since the days of their fathers","Only since the return from Babylon","Since the reign of David","Since the days of Noah"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:7",verse:"Since the days of our fathers have we been in a great trespass unto this day; and for our iniquities have we, our kings, and our priests, been delivered into the hand of the kings of the lands, to the sword, to captivity, and to a spoil, and to confusion of face, as it is this day.", insight:"This was no new failure but an old pattern. Ezra faced the family history honestly."},
{q:"What had their iniquities cost them?", options:["They, their kings, and their priests had been delivered into the hand of the kings of the lands","They had been made rulers over Persia","They had lost nothing","They had grown wealthy"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:7",verse:"Since the days of our fathers have we been in a great trespass unto this day; and for our iniquities have we, our kings, and our priests, been delivered into the hand of the kings of the lands, to the sword, to captivity, and to a spoil, and to confusion of face, as it is this day.", insight:"Sin had cost them sword, captivity, and shame. Ezra remembered what disobedience buys."},
{q:"What grace had God shown 'for a little space'?", options:["To leave them a remnant to escape, and to give them a nail in his holy place","To make them kings over the nations","To give them all the earth","To end every sorrow"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:8",verse:"And now for a little space grace hath been shewed from the LORD our God, to leave us a remnant to escape, and to give us a nail in his holy place, that our God may lighten our eyes, and give us a little reviving in our bondage.", insight:"A remnant, a nail, a little reviving: small mercies, but they were everything. God keeps a foothold for His people."},
{q:"How does Ezra describe God's mercy in their bondage?", options:["God had not forsaken them in bondage, but extended mercy in the sight of the kings of Persia","God had broken every chain in a single night","God had sent fire from heaven","God had closed the temple doors"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:9",verse:"For we were bondmen; yet our God hath not forsaken us in our bondage, but hath extended mercy unto us in the sight of the kings of Persia, to give us a reviving, to set up the house of our God, and to repair the desolations thereof, and to give us a wall in Judah and in Jerusalem.", insight:"Even in bondage, they were not abandoned. God's mercy followed them into captivity and out again."},
{q:"What does Ezra say after recounting God's mercies?", options:["What shall we say after this? for we have forsaken thy commandments","We have kept thy law perfectly","We need no further mercy","Let us return to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:10",verse:"And now, O our God, what shall we say after this? for we have forsaken thy commandments,", insight:"Mercy received makes sin look worse, not better. Gratitude deepens repentance."},
{q:"How had the prophets described the land?", options:["An unclean land with the filthiness of the people of the lands","A land flowing with milk and honey","A land of peace and rest","A land God had forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:11",verse:"Which thou hast commanded by thy servants the prophets, saying, The land, unto which ye go to possess it, is an unclean land with the filthiness of the people of the lands, with their abominations, which have filled it from one end to another with their uncleanness.", insight:"God had warned them plainly through His prophets. The command was old, and the disobedience was willful."},
{q:"What command about marriage had God given?", options:["Give not your daughters unto their sons, neither take their daughters unto your sons","Marry only within the walls of Jerusalem","Take wives from every nation","Remain unmarried all your days"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:12",verse:"Now therefore give not your daughters unto their sons, neither take their daughters unto your sons, nor seek their peace or their wealth for ever: that ye may be strong, and eat the good of the land, and leave it for an inheritance to your children for ever.", insight:"The command protected their faith, not their bloodline. Unequal unions pull hearts away from God."},
{q:"What would obedience bring?", options:["That they might be strong, and eat the good of the land, and leave it for an inheritance to their children forever","Riches in Babylon","Long life in Persia","Victory over every enemy"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:12",verse:"Now therefore give not your daughters unto their sons, neither take their daughters unto your sons, nor seek their peace or their wealth for ever: that ye may be strong, and eat the good of the land, and leave it for an inheritance to your children for ever.", insight:"Obedience was the path to strength and inheritance. God's commands carry God's blessings."},
{q:"What does Ezra say about God's punishment?", options:["God had punished them less than their iniquities deserved","God had punished them too severely","God had not punished them at all","God's punishment was finished forever"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:13",verse:"And after all that is come upon us for our evil deeds, and for our great trespass, seeing that thou our God hast punished us less than our iniquities deserve, and hast given us such deliverance as this;", insight:"Less than we deserve: that is the definition of mercy. Ezra knew it and said it plainly."},
{q:"How does Ezra end his prayer?", options:["O LORD God of Israel, thou art righteous: we cannot stand before thee because of this","With a song of triumph","With a blessing on the princes","With a vow to build the wall"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 9:15",verse:"O LORD God of Israel, thou art righteous: for we remain yet escaped, as it is this day: behold, we are before thee in our trespasses: for we cannot stand before thee because of this.", insight:"He ends where all true prayer ends: God is righteous, and we are not. There is no defense, only mercy."}
]},
{n:10, name:"A Solemn Covenant", seal:{ref:"Ezra 10:4",verse:"Arise; for this matter belongeth unto thee: we also will be with thee: be of good courage, and do it."}, facts:["Did you know the whole painful process lasted about three months, from the twentieth day of the ninth month to the first day of the first month?","Did you know it was pouring rain as the people stood trembling in the open street before the house of God?","Did you know the guilty are listed by name, beginning with the priests, because the leaders were examined first?"], questions:[
{q:"What was Ezra doing when the great congregation assembled to him?", options:["He had prayed and confessed, weeping and casting himself down before the house of God","He was reading the law to the people","He was offering the evening sacrifice","He was numbering the Levites"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:1",verse:"Now when Ezra had prayed, and when he had confessed, weeping and casting himself down before the house of God, there assembled unto him out of Israel a very great congregation of men and women and children: for the people wept very sore.", insight:"Private brokenness became public revival. One man's tears gathered a nation."},
{q:"Who first spoke up with a proposal?", options:["Shechaniah the son of Jehiel, one of the sons of Elam","Ezra the priest","Tatnai the governor","Johanan the son of Eliashib"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:2",verse:"And Shechaniah the son of Jehiel, one of the sons of Elam, answered and said unto Ezra, We have trespassed against our God, and have taken strange wives of the people of the land: yet now there is hope in Israel concerning this thing.", insight:"God raised up a voice from among the guilty themselves. Repentance often starts with 'we have trespassed.'"},
{q:"What hope did Shechaniah declare?", options:["Yet now there is hope in Israel concerning this thing","There is no hope left for us","Our only hope is in Babylon","Hope must wait for another generation"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:2",verse:"And Shechaniah the son of Jehiel, one of the sons of Elam, answered and said unto Ezra, We have trespassed against our God, and have taken strange wives of the people of the land: yet now there is hope in Israel concerning this thing.", insight:"Even in deep trespass, hope was not dead. Where there is confession, there is hope."},
{q:"What covenant did they propose to make?", options:["To put away all the strange wives, and such as are born of them, according to the counsel of those that tremble at God's commandment","To rebuild the walls of Jerusalem","To pay double tribute to the king","To return to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:3",verse:"Now therefore let us make a covenant with our God to put away all the wives, and such as are born of them, according to the counsel of my lord, and of those that tremble at the commandment of our God; and let it be done according to the law.", insight:"The remedy matched the sin exactly. True repentance puts away what caused the fall."},
{q:"What charge did Shechaniah give to Ezra?", options:["Arise; for this matter belongeth unto thee: be of good courage, and do it","Flee to Egypt for safety","Wait for a letter from the king","Do nothing until spring"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:4",verse:"Arise; for this matter belongeth unto thee: we also will be with thee: be of good courage, and do it.", insight:"Leadership means standing up when the matter belongs to you. Courage is obedience in action."},
{q:"What did Ezra make the chief priests, the Levites, and all Israel swear?", options:["That they should do according to this word","That they would pay the temple tax","That they would serve in the king's army","That they would keep the Passover"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:5",verse:"Then arose Ezra, and made the chief priests, the Levites, and all Israel, to swear that they should do according to this word. And they sware.", insight:"A solemn oath sealed the resolve. Vows made before God give backbone to good intentions."},
{q:"Where did Ezra go to mourn, eating no bread and drinking no water?", options:["Into the chamber of Johanan the son of Eliashib","Into the king's palace","To the river Ahava","To his house in Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:6",verse:"Then Ezra rose up from before the house of God, and went into the chamber of Johanan the son of Eliashib: and when he came thither, he did eat no bread, nor drink water: for he mourned because of the transgression of them that had been carried away.", insight:"Ezra's mourning was as serious as the sin. He would not feast while God's people were in trespass."},
{q:"What proclamation went throughout Judah and Jerusalem?", options:["That all the children of the captivity should gather themselves together unto Jerusalem","That the temple was finished","That the king was coming to visit","That a new fast be proclaimed"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:7",verse:"And they made proclamation throughout Judah and Jerusalem unto all the children of the captivity, that they should gather themselves together unto Jerusalem;", insight:"The call went out to everyone. Corporate sin requires a corporate gathering."},
{q:"What awaited any who would not come within three days?", options:["All his substance forfeited, and himself separated from the congregation","He would be sold as a slave","He would pay a heavy fine","He would be forgiven anyway"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:8",verse:"And that whosoever would not come within three days, according to the counsel of the princes and the elders, all his substance should be forfeited, and himself separated from the congregation of those that had been carried away.", insight:"The stakes were real: property and belonging. Discipline protects the community's holiness."},
{q:"When did the men of Judah and Benjamin gather at Jerusalem?", options:["In the ninth month, on the twentieth day of the month","In the first month, on the first day","In the seventh month, at the feast","In the tenth month, on the first day"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:9",verse:"Then all the men of Judah and Benjamin gathered themselves together unto Jerusalem within three days. It was the ninth month, on the twentieth day of the month; and all the people sat in the street of the house of God, trembling because of this matter, and for the great rain.", insight:"Three days was all it took for the nation to assemble. Urgency marked their repentance."},
{q:"Why were the people trembling as they sat in the street of the house of God?", options:["Because of this matter, and for the great rain","Because of an earthquake","Because the king's army approached","Because of a famine"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:9",verse:"Then all the men of Judah and Benjamin gathered themselves together unto Jerusalem within three days. It was the ninth month, on the twentieth day of the month; and all the people sat in the street of the house of God, trembling because of this matter, and for the great rain.", insight:"Conviction and cold rain together: they trembled body and soul. God's presence was felt in the conscience and in the weather."},
{q:"What did Ezra the priest say when he stood up?", options:["Ye have transgressed, and have taken strange wives, to increase the trespass of Israel","Ye have kept the law faithfully","Ye have built the walls well","Ye have remembered the Passover"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:10",verse:"And Ezra the priest stood up, and said unto them, Ye have transgressed, and have taken strange wives, to increase the trespass of Israel.", insight:"Ezra named the sin plainly. Vague guilt never leads to real repentance."},
{q:"What did Ezra command them to do?", options:["Make confession unto the LORD, and separate yourselves from the people of the land and from the strange wives","Offer a thousand bullocks","Return to Babylon at once","Build new houses in Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:11",verse:"Now therefore make confession unto the LORD God of your fathers, and do his pleasure: and separate yourselves from the people of the land, and from the strange wives.", insight:"Confession first, then separation. Right words must be followed by right action."},
{q:"How did all the congregation answer?", options:["With a loud voice: As thou hast said, so must we do","In guilty silence","They refused and walked away","They asked for the king's ruling"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:12",verse:"Then all the congregation answered and said with a loud voice, As thou hast said, so must we do.", insight:"A loud, united yes. When God's word is clear, the people's answer should be too."},
{q:"Why could the matter not be finished in a day or two?", options:["The people were many, it was a time of much rain, and many had transgressed","The priests were too few","The king had forbidden haste","There was no money for the work"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:13",verse:"But the people are many, and it is a time of much rain, and we are not able to stand without, neither is this a work of one day or two: for we are many that have transgressed in this thing.", insight:"They were honest about the scale of the task. Real repentance plans carefully, not hastily."},
{q:"What plan did they agree upon?", options:["Let the rulers stand, and let offenders come at appointed times with their city's elders and judges","Let everyone leave the city at once","Let the guilty be stoned","Let the matter be forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:14",verse:"Let now our rulers of all the congregation stand, and let all them which have taken strange wives in our cities come at appointed times, and with them the elders of every city, and the judges thereof, until the fierce wrath of our God for this matter be turned from us.", insight:"Orderly process, city by city. Even painful discipline was done decently and in order."},
{q:"Who was appointed to oversee the examination?", options:["Jonathan the son of Asahel and Jahaziah the son of Tikvah, with Meshullam and Shabbethai the Levite helping them","Ezra alone","Officers of the Persian king","The Nethinims"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:15",verse:"Only Jonathan the son of Asahel and Jahaziah the son of Tikvah were employed about this matter: and Meshullam and Shabbethai the Levite helped them.", insight:"Trusted men were named for a hard task. Difficult work needs designated, accountable hands."},
{q:"When did they sit down to examine the matter?", options:["On the first day of the tenth month","On the first day of the seventh month","On the twentieth day of the ninth month","On the first day of the first month"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:16",verse:"And the children of the captivity did so. And Ezra the priest, with certain chief of the fathers, after the house of their fathers, and all of them by their names, were separated, and sat down in the first day of the tenth month to examine the matter.", insight:"They began as agreed, on the appointed day. Faithfulness keeps its appointments."},
{q:"When did they make an end with all the men who had taken strange wives?", options:["By the first day of the first month","By the Passover","By the feast of tabernacles","The work was never finished"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:17",verse:"And they made an end with all the men that had taken strange wives by the first day of the first month.", insight:"About three months of painful, thorough work. They finished what they started."},
{q:"What did the guilty priests do?", options:["They gave their hands that they would put away their wives, and offered a ram for their trespass","They gave silver and departed","They offered doves and stayed","They refused and were cast out"], a:0, ref:"Ezra 10:19",verse:"And they gave their hands that they would put away their wives; and being guilty, they offered a ram of the flock for their trespass.", insight:"A handshake of commitment and a ram for atonement: the leaders led in repentance first."}
]}
]);
